## Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are considered valuable materials for many applications such as gas storage, separation, catalysis, and sensing.[1] In addition, MOFs are proven to be suitable precursor materials for the production of useful metal oxide and carbon materials for the utilization in catalytic reaction, imaging, and energy storage.[2] The MOFs’ properties are normally determined by their components and structural features. Furthermore, the morphological features of MOFs are also important for fine-tuning their properties. Eventually, the compositional, structural, and morphological features of MOFs will determine their properties and consequently, their applications. Therefore, controlling the formation of MOFs from the perspective of their components, structure, and morphology is of utmost significance for the construction of well-designed MOFs with desired properties.
Within this context, the preparation of hybrid MOFs, which have several different components or structures in one particle, is a vital strategy for the production of MOFs with well-controlled properties. The hybrid MOFs often display advanced properties compared to pure monomeric MOFs; moreover, the limitations of pure MOFs can be overcome by incorporating them into hybrid forms.[3] Hybrid MOFs can be constructed by integrating MOF materials with other functional materials such as silica, magnetic particles, polystyrene beads, or noble metal particles[4] to provide new capabilities, which cannot be directly achieved with pure MOFs. Hybrid MOFs can also be constructed by the conjugation of MOFs with other kinds of MOFs. The MOF-onMOF growth is an excellent route to conjugate more than two MOFs for the production of well-defined hybrid MOFs.[5] However, deeper insight into MOF-on-MOF growth is still required for controlled MOF-on-MOF growth and thus, for the production of well-designed hybrid MOFs. Herein, we report a unique construction route for atypical hybrid MOF particles. Three distinctive processes were investigated for the controlled production of core–shell-type hybrid MOF rings and plates: 1) MOF-on-MOF growth of 3D ZIF on a ZIF-L template, 2) etching of a part of the 2D ZIF-L template, and 3) structural transformation of the 2D ZIF-L template to 3D ZIF. Extensive data on the structure, morphology, and composition changes of MOF particles during the reactions confirmed the simultaneous and combinative progress of three processes, depending upon the amounts and kinds of reactants. In addition, comprehensive monitoring and analysis of the structure, morphology, and composition changes of MOF particles were performed to elucidate the elaborate route for the production of hybrid MOF particles with unique morphology and composition.
## Results and Discussion
The structural transformation of 2D layered ZIF-L $\{[Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{M e}|\mathsf{m})_{2}(\mathsf{H M e}|\mathsf{m})_{1/2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{3/2}]_{n},$ Scheme 1 and Figure S1} to 3D ZIF-8 $\{[Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{M e l m})_{2}]_{n}$ , Scheme 1 and Figure S1} via a simple thermal treatment of leaf-shaped ZIF-L has recently been reported.[6] During the thermal treatment of ZIF-L, a thermodynamically unstable 2D ZIF-L was transformed to more stable 3D ZIF-8 while maintaining its original leaf shape (Scheme 1, T process). Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of a series of samples obtained at different time points during the thermal treatment indicated the gradual structural transformation of 2D ZIF-L to 3D ZIF-8 (Figure S2). The corresponding scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images revealed that there was no significant morphological change, including no thickness change during the structural transformation (Figure S3). The morphology of the structurally transformed 3D ZIF-8 is a good evidence for the crystal-tocrystal transformation of ZIF-L to ZIF-8. The transformed 3D ZIF8 has a leaf shape, which is exactly identical to the original ZIF-L. If there is a decomposition and reconstruction process during the ZIF-L to ZIF-8 transformation, the final ZIF-8 should have a favorable dodecahedron shape of ZIF-8 instead of a leaf shape.
The PXRD pattern of the core–shell rings revealed that they consisted of a pure 3D ZIF phase without a 2D ZIF-L phase (Figure 1c). The characteristic PXRD peaks for 2D ZIF-L entirely disappeared, and a PXRD pattern was identical to that of 3D ZIF. No detection of a 2D ZIF phase suggested two clear and specific conclusions. The first is the complete transformation of the initial 2D ZIF-L to 3D ZIF, and the other is that the additionally grown MOF was a 3D ZIF. The infrared (IR) spectrum of the core–shell rings confirmed again the presence of a pure 3D ZIF phase without a 2D ZIF-L phase. The IR spectrum (Figure S6i) was perfectly identical to that of 3D ZIF (Figure S6j). Particularly, the characteristic in-plane-bending at $1101~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ originated from the free HMeIm[8] presenting within 2D ZIF-L (Figure S6a) completely disappeared after the reaction. These core–shell rings can be generated through the unique combination of three simultaneous processes (GET process, Scheme 1): 1) additional MOF-on-MOF growth of ZIF-67 from ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and HMeIm present within the reaction solution (G process), 2) etching at the center of the template (E process), and 3) structural transformation of the ZIF-L template to a ZIF-8 core (T process). As already stated, the additional growth of the secondary MOF was firstly evidenced by two morphological changes: a thickness increase (Figure 1b) and a change in the edge shape (Figure 1b). In addition to these morphological evidences, the energydispersive X-ray (EDX) spectrum of the product indicated the formation of a hybrid MOF incorporating both a Zn-containing MOF and a Co-containing MOF, as evidenced by the detection of both Zn and Co (Figure 1d). Furthermore, elemental mapping images of a core–shell ring revealed the distribution of Zn and Co throughout the particle (Figure 1f). The signal from Zn was slightly stronger at the center with a leaf-shape, owing to the generation of a ZIF-8 core via the structural transformation of leaf-shaped ZIF-L. In contrast, the shell part of a ring was dominated by Co, owing to the additional growth of MOF from an abundance of HMeIm and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ present in the reaction solution. However, the shell part also contained some $Z n^{2+}$ , which resulted in the formation of a bimetallic 3D ZIF shell with a majority of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and a minority of $Z n^{2+}$ [denoted as ZIF-67(Co, Zn)] instead of a pure ZIF-67 shell. The bimetallic ZIF-67(Co, Zn) shell was formed because the $Z n^{2+}$ ions released during the etching of the initial ZIF-L template were also involved in the additional growth of the secondary MOF to form the shell part. Lastly, the EDX line scanning data clearly confirmed the formation of well-defined core–shell-type ZIF-8@ZIF-67(Co, Zn), as evinced by the characteristic patterns for the core–shell structure (Figure 1e). The intensity for Co was stronger at the edge of the particle, as it is a main component of the shell part of ZIF-67(Co, Zn). Zn was abundant at the middle of the particle as a component of the ZIF-8 core, but also detected from the edge of the particle as a minor component of the ZIF-67(Co, Zn) shell.
The detailed formation mechanism of the atypical core– shell rings was investigated by analysing a series of samples acquired at different time points during the reaction. First, SEM images of the samples revealed that MOF-on-MOF growth commenced from the outside of the particles at the early stage of the reaction (Figure 2c). The SEM image acquired at $^\textrm{\scriptsize3h}$ indicates a clear shape change, from rounded leaf to an angled elongated hexagon; this signifies the additional growth of the secondary 3D ZIF at the edge of the particle. Furthermore, the particle thickness increased with increasing reaction time owing to the growth of the secondary ZIF shell (Figure S7). Second, etching occurred to a greater extent at the border between a newly grown MOF and a bare template MOF, especially at both ends of the particle (Figures 2f and g), which eventually resulted in a hole at the center of the particle (Figures 2h and i). In general, MOF materials are etched (or decomposed) in an acidic environment through the re-protonation of ligands incorporated within the framework.[9,10] The solutions containing transition metal ions normally generate the protons through the hydrolysis and so provide an acidic environment.[10] ZIF-L can be chemically etched by the protons generated from the hydrolysis of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ . This combinatory process (GE process) of the
secondary MOF growth and the etching resulted in unique core– shell-type hybrid rings. Third, PXRD patterns of a series of samples were also measured to relate the structural transformation with its morphological change (Figures 2a-i). The PXRD data indicated the gradual structural transformation of the 2D ZIF-L template to 3D ZIF-8 (T process). The transformation was almost completed at approximately $18\mathrm{~h~}$ . The IR spectra of the samples also confirmed that the structural transformation of 2D ZIF-L to 3D ZIF was completed at approximately 18 h (Figure S6). It is well known that 3D ZIF is more chemically stable than 2D ZIF-L.[11] In special, the acidic stability of 2D ZIF-L is quite lower than that of 3D ZIF.[11b-d] Without the transformation of 2D ZIF-L to stable 3D ZIF-8, ZIF-L will be completely etched, and so no formation of core–shell hybrid MOF of ZIF-8@ZIF-67. Finally, the GET process was completed to produce core–shell-type rings of ZIF-8@ZIF-67(Co, Zn) from a ZIF-L template (Figure 2j).
To find out the cause for the unexpected etching process, two control experiments were performed. In the first control experiment, a similar thermal treatment of leaf-shaped 2D ZIF-L in the presence of only HMeIm was conducted. First of all, no morphological change was detected during this reaction, even after $36\textsf{h}$ (Figure S9). Furthermore, the PXRD patterns of the samples revealed that the transformation of the 2D ZIF-L template to 3D ZIF-8 was not complete even after $36\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure S9d). In the second control experiment, a thermal treatment of 2D ZIF-L in the presence of $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ was conducted. First, SEM images of the product revealed several morphological changes including the formation of rings (Figure S10b). We can thus conclude that the added $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ induced the etching (E process) of ZIF-L. Moreover, a slight thickness increase (Figure S10b) was observed, indicating the additional growth of a small amount of 3D ZIF (g process). The trivial shape change from the rounded edge to the slightly angled edge also indicated a low degree of additional growth of 3D ZIF from the MeIm- released during the etching of the initial ZIF-L, and from an excess amount of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ present and a small amount of $Z n^{2+}$ released during the etching. Indeed, the EDX spectrum of the product indicated that a small amount of Co was present within the final product (Figure S13f; the ratio of Zn:Co was found to be 1:0.13). Furthermore, elemental mapping images clearly indicated the incorporation of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ within the final MOF ring (Figure S10e), and EDX line scanning data also supported the low-degree growth of ZIF-67, as evinced by the weak detection of Co (Figure S10d). Second, the PXRD pattern of the resulting MOF rings clearly indicated the structural transformation of 2D ZIF-L to 3D ZIF-8 (T process), as characteristic PXRD peaks for 3D ZIF were only detected (Figure S10c). Furthermore, the characteristic in-plane-bending at $1101~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for 2D ZIF-L was absent in the IR spectrum after the reaction (Figure S12).
For a comprehensive understanding of the formation of MOF rings through the gET process, the overall process was monitored by analysing samples acquired at different time points. Etching began at a very early stage of the reaction, and was almost complete in 1 h, as shown in SEM images (Figure S11). Due to the etching process at the early stage of the reaction, intermediates with a thin layer at the particle center were observed in the sample obtained at $0.5{\mathrm{~h~}}$ . In particular, the particle became thinner, except at the very outside, where a low degree of 3D ZIF growth was already in progress, and the hole generation had been initiated at the border between the thick and thin parts. A minor shape change from a rounded edge to a slightly angled edge was also observed at the early stage of the reaction $(0.5\mathsf{h})$ owing to the additional growth of a small amount of the 3D ZIF shell (Figure S11b). The detection of Co in the EDX spectrum of the sample obtained at $0.5{\ h}$ , suggesting the incorporation of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ at the early stage of the reaction via the shell growth (Figure S13b; the ratio of $Z n:\mathsf{C o}$ was found to be 1:0.05 in this sample). The characteristic PXRD peaks of the initial 2D ZIF-L were considerably weakened at even the sample obtained at $0.5{\ h}$ , and the structural transformation of 2D ZIF-L to 3D ZIF was almost complete at 2 h (Figure S11d). These observations indicated that the transformation was accelerated in the presence of $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ .
Next, with a purpose for studying the GT process (Scheme 1; a rapid growth of ZIF-67 on the surface of the template before the substantial etching of the template, and thus producing a core–shell-type plate of $Z|\mathsf{F}{-}8@\mathsf{Z}|\mathsf{F}{-}67$ without a hole), a similar thermal treatment of 2D ZIF-L was carried out in the presence of more excess amounts of $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and HMeIm. Indeed, core– shell-type elongated hexagonal plates without holes were produced from the above reaction (Figure 3). The resulting product was significantly thicker than the initial ZIF-L template, indicating significant growth of the secondary MOF (Figure S14f). The outer shape of the particles also changed from a rounded edge to an angled edge, indicating also the additional growth of 3D ZIF. The PXRD pattern (Figure 3f) and IR spectrum (Figure S15f) of the product revealed the presence of only a 3D ZIF phase without a 2D ZIF phase. Elemental mapping images of ZIF-8@ZIF-67 plate clearly showed a core–shell structure consisting of a ZIF-8 core with a rounded edge and a ZIF-67 shell with an angled edge (Figure 3g). Zn was dominant at the center of the particle as a part of the core component (transformed ZIF-8) with an initial leaf shape. On the other hand, Co was detected throughout the particle, but more strongly detected at the edge of the particle as a component of the shell (newly grown ZIF-67). In addition, the EDX line scanning data (Figures 3h and i) clearly confirmed the well-defined core–shell structure (trace amount of Zn was detected in the shell part because a trace amount of $Z n^{2+}$ released from the trivial etching of ZIF-L was incorporated during the growth). SEM images of a series of samples indicated that the thickness of the particles increased as the reaction time increased (Figure S14). The SEM images also revealed that the MOF growth commenced from the outside of the template, and the newly grown MOF covered almost the entire template at $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ before any significant etching took place (Figure 3). The PXRD pattern of the sample acquired at 6 h indicated that the 2D to 3D transformation was not complete at that time, but there had been no significant etching of 2D ZIF, possibly because the covered ZIF-67 shell protected the core from being etched. In contrast, etching was observed to have occurred at 6 h during the formation of core–shell rings (Figure 2). PXRD patterns and IR spectra of these samples revealed that the transformation of 2D ZIF to 3D ZIF was almost complete at approximately 9 h (Figures 3 and S15).
As an alternative route for the production of core–shelltype elongated hexagonal plates of ZIF-8@ZIF-67, the solvothermal reaction of leaf-shaped ZIF-8 in the presence of $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and HMeIm was conducted; for this, the leaf-shaped ZIF-8 was first prepared from the transformation of the leafshaped ZIF-L (Scheme 1, T process followed by G process). As expected, a well-defined core–shell-type hybrid of ZIF-8@ZIF-67 without a hole was produced (Figure S17). The product displayed a significant thickness increase and a characteristic angled edge due to the MOF growth. The PXRD pattern of the product revealed a pure 3D ZIF phase (Figure S17d). In addition, elemental mapping images and elemental line scanning profile data indicated that no $Z n^{2+}$ was involved during the MOF growth; therefore, no Zn was present in the shell portion (Figure S18). This result indicated that the 3D ZIF-8 template was not etched, although etching distinctly occurred on the 2D ZIF-L template.
A thermal treatment on 2D ZIF-L was conducted, in this time in the presence of extra $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}$ and HMeIm, to verify the formation of rings of Z $[1]{-8\textcircled{\times}21}\vert\mathsf{F}{-}8$ through GET process (Scheme 1). Indeed, rings of ZIF-8@ZIF-8 were generated from the reaction (Figure 4a). First, the particle thickness increased due to the additional growth of ZIF-8 (Figure S20), and the angled edge of the product was further evidence for the growth of 3D ZIF-8 (Figure 4a). Furthermore, SEM images of the samples revealed that etching was initiated from both ends of the particles and propagated toward the middle of the particles (Figure S19). The solution containing $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}$ generates the protons through the hydrolysis and so provides an acidic environment for the etching of ZIF-L.[9,10] A series of PXRD patterns confirmed the structural transformation of 2D template to 3D ZIF and the growth of 3D ZIF-8, as showing a pure 3D phase (Figure S19). $\mathsf{A}$ similar thermal treatment of 2D ZIF-L in the presence of only $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}$ without extra HMeIm was also conducted. This reaction resulted in well-defined rings within a relatively short time, (Figure 4b). In fact, the rings were produced from the combination of only two dominant processes (Scheme 1; ET process): etching of 2D ZIF-L and structural transformation to ZIF-8 with no significant MOF-on-MOF growth. The most 2D ZIF-L template was etched in $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}$ solution except the part transformed to stable 3D ZIF-8 (Figure 4b). The combination of the partial transformation of unstable 2D ZIF-L to stable 3D ZIF8 at the edge of the particles and the partial etching of unstable 2D ZIF-L at the center of the particles during the reaction makes it possible to form the ring-type MOF. The SEM images displayed no significant change in the edge shape and the structural transformation of template was almost complete at 1 h, as shown in (Figure S21).
Carbon-based oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) catalysts,[12,13] are considered to replace expensive Pt-based catalysts. In particular, the preparation of carbon-based ORR catalysts from the pyrolysis of MOFs is quite intriguing, because the composition and structure of MOFs can be straightforwardly tuned, thereby allowing the convenient tuning of the catalytic activity of the resulting carbon-based materials.[13] Therefore, we investigated the ORR activities of carbon-based materials prepared from the pyrolysis of the resulting hybrid MOF rings and plates. First, the core–shell-type rings and plates of ZIF8@ZIF-67 were pyrolyzed at $800^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The monomeric counterparts, ZIF-L, ZIF-8, and ZIF-67, were also pyrolyzed under the same conditions for comparison. SEM images of the pyrolyzed carbon-based materials (denoted as Cring and C-plate) revealed that the ring and plate morphologies of hybrid MOFs were well-maintained even after the pyrolysis (Figures 5 and S24). Thin flat morphological feature of materials known to provide many active sites and effective contact with the reactants related to ORR.[14] Furthermore, high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images displayed CNTs, graphitic carbons, and Co nanoparticles within the resulting C-ring and C-plate (Figures 5 and S24). The lattice fringe images of C-ring and C-plate revealed the formation of graphitic carbon and metallic Co, as displaying the characteristic $d-$ spacing of $0.36~\mathsf{n m}$ representing the (002) plane of graphitic carbon and the $d-$ spacing of 0.21 nm representing the (111) plane of metallic Co. In addition, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) data (Figures 5c and S32) revealed the presence of active N and Co species[15] known to enhance the ORR activity. All of C-ring and C-plate as well as C-ZIF-L, C-ZIF8, and C-ZIF-67 acquired from the pyrolysis of ZIF-L, ZIF-8, and ZIF-67 were fully characterized by SEM, TEM, PXRD, Raman spectroscopy, and XPS (see Figures S23-S32 for detail).
The ORR activity of the products was first tested using cyclic voltammetry (CV) $\mathsf{i n}$ a 0.1 M KOH electrolyte with a threeelectrode system. The CV curves of samples measured in the $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ -saturated electrolyte displayed a well-defined cathodic peak, indicating their electrocatalytic activity for the ORR (Figures 5d and S33). However, there was no significant cathodic peak in the CV curves measured in a ${\sf N}_{2}$ -saturated electrolyte. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) data were also collected for the samples using a rotating disk electrode (RDE) at 1600 rpm (Figure 5e). Notably, more positive half-wave potentials $(E_{1/2}$ 0.84 and 0.83 V) and higher diffusion-limited current densities $(j_{I}$ at $0.4~\mathsf{V}$ ; -4.68 and $-4.65~\mathsf{m A}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2})$ were obtained from C-ring and C-plate, respectively. In contrast, the half-wave potentials and diffusion-limited current densities of C-ZIF-L, C-ZIF-8, and C-ZIF-67 were found to be 0.59, 0.63, and $_{0.82\mathrm{~V~}}$ , and -1.89, - 2.37, and $-2.70\quad\mathsf{m A}\quad\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , respectively. In addition, the electrochemical stabilities of the resulting carbon products were tested by measuring its current–time chronoamperometric response in ORR at $0.6\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figure 5f). C-ZIF-L, C-ZIF-8, and CZIF-67 displayed a huge current loss and approximately 49, 54, and $80\%$ relative currents remained, respectively, after a long time operation for $12\mathsf{h}$ . However, C-ring and C-plate showed an excellent electrochemical stability as demonstrated by a perseverance of $97\%$ relative current after operating for $12\ h$ (Figure 5f). The ORR activities of C-ring and C-plate are comparable to that of commercial $\mathsf{P t/C}$ and the electrochemical stabilities of C-ring and C-plate are much better than that of Pt/C (Figures 5e and f). In addition, the excellent ORR activities of Cring and C-plate are confirmed by comparing it with other recently developed MOF-derived carbon materials (Table S1). These enhanced ORR activities of the carbon materials obtained from the core–shell-type hybrid MOFs can be attributed to their unique compositional and morphological features, such as substantial Co and N doping within the resulting carbon materials, and relatively thin and flat morphology, which originate from the compositional and morphological features of the core–shell-type hybrid MOF rings and plates of ZIF-8@ZIF67.
## Conclusion
In conclusion, controlling the combination of three distinct processes (G: growth, E: etching, and T: transformation) makes it possible to produce unique hybrid rings and plates of MOFs from the ZIF-L starting material. For example, GET process resulted in the rings of ZIF-8@ZIF-67(Co, Zn) and ZIF-8@ZIF-8; GT process resulted in the plates of ZIF-8@ZIF-67; and ET process resulted in the rings of ZIF-8. In particular, monitoring the formation routes of hybrid rings and plates using electron microscopy provided visual evidence of the progress of MOF-onMOF growth and etching. In addition, tracking the reaction through X-ray diffraction and elemental analysis confirmed the structural transformation and MOF-on-MOF growth. Eventually, well-defined atypical hybrid MOFs were produced by controlling the combination of three distinct processes through regulating progress of each process by controlling the reaction conditions. As a proof-of-concept, carbon materials prepared from the pyrolysis of hybrid MOF rings and plates displayed enhanced ORR activities compared to their monomeric counterparts, because the unique composition and morphology of hybrid
MOFs impart desirable compositional and morphological features to the resulting carbon materials for the ORR.
